@*
    Razor parity twin -- composed-page (layout) (ledger E8/E9).

    Razor is now measured on ALL EIGHT workloads. It previously existed for composed-page only,
    which meant the repository's "faster than Razor" claim rested on the single least favourable
    workload in the set. An engine with one workload does not belong in a cross-stack table.

    Every value comes from the shared PUBLIC fixtures under src/Models/, the same objects every
    other engine reads, so this twin cannot drift from the engine it is compared against. Its
    predecessor did drift -- it carried a hand-duplicated 56 KB copy of the area dictionary that had
    diverged unnoticed -- precisely because nothing compared the two.

    Reproduces the byte sequence Heddle's home.heddle + layout.heddle actually emit -- the
    reusable-section defaults, the fixed component outputs and the ordered area menus -- rather
    than transcribing the layout's literal HTML chrome. See GoldenCorpus/README.md for why
    Heddle's output is that fragment sequence.
*@
@using Heddle.Benchmarks.Dotnet.Models
@{
    var sections = TwinContent.Sections();
    var comp = TwinContent.Components();
    var areas = TwinContent.Areas;
    var order = TwinContent.AreaOrder;
}
@Html.Raw(sections["meta"])@Html.Raw(sections["social"])@Html.Raw(comp["assets_styles"])@Html.Raw(comp["custom_styles"])@Html.Raw(comp["head_scripts"])@Html.Raw(comp["body_scripts"])@for (var i = 0; i < order.Length - 1; i++)
{@Html.Raw(areas[order[i]])}@*
    layout.heddle's @body() slot sits here, between the last navigation area and the footer area.
    It resolves to the layout's empty default, so this renders nothing -- but the slot is kept in
    its real position, and Razor requires a layout to call RenderBody exactly once.
*@@RenderBody()@Html.Raw(areas[order[order.Length - 1]])@Html.Raw(comp["assets_scripts"])@Html.Raw(sections["page_scripts"])@Html.Raw(sections["endpage_scripts"])@Html.Raw(comp["body_end_scripts"])
